index 3b284419..0894a1b5 100644
--- i/docs/backlog.md
+++ w/docs/backlog.md
@@ -1,6 +1,6 @@
 # DevDesk Backlog

-**Last Updated:** 2026-11-16
+**Last Updated:** 2026-11-17

 Open work for DevDesk: known defects, technical debt, and planned features.
 Replaces the former `todo.md` at the repository root. Items completed there
@@ -98,6 +98,8 @@ decided together and fixed in one pass; see [§1.2](#12-the-five-parked-defects)

 ### 1.1 Fixed

+**D158 — DEVDESK-505 (NX-02a): the bench seeds a registry upstream inside its namespace, fed by a dataset generated in Go and pushed by skopeo, and cuts it by scaling to zero. Decided (user, archived 2026-11-17; plan `.claude/plans/DEVDESK-505-plan.md`, drafted there under this number; Q1 to Q4 settled as recommended, except where stated): (1) The client is the host's `skopeo` (1.18.0 when planned; not pinned by mise), run as a process by `skopeo.go` with a cleaned environment and an explicit policy; a missing binary is `SkopeoMissing`. The bench does not measure Nexus with the client that populated it. (2) The images are generated in Go at seed time as OCI layouts in a temporary directory: uncompressed layer tar, sorted entries, zero timestamps, no `created` in the config, so the digests are stable and pinned in a golden file. A `layerGzip` constant switches the layer to gzip, without name or date, if Nexus refuses uncompressed layers (H-LAYER). Nothing is committed as a binary and nothing comes from the network. (3) The upstream is a `registry:3.0.0` Deployment `bench-upstream` in the namespace, with PVC `bench-upstream-data` (kept across a cut, deleted only by `reset`) and a ClusterIP Service `bench-upstream:5000`. The Deployment manifest carries no `replicas`, so `kubectl scale` owns that field and server-side apply never fights it: a cut scales to 0, a restore to 1. The development machine reaches the upstream by `kubectl port-forward` on loopback, for the seed only. No NodePort: a writable registry is not exposed to the LAN. The PVC is read by name, not by label (this corrects a latent NX-01 bug). (4) The hosted repository is seeded through path routing on the HTTP port (`127.0.0.1:<HTTPNodePort>/docker-hosted/...`). Path routing is off by default on Nexus 3.98.1 (`docker.pathEnabled` is null and `/v2/` answers 404 on the HTTP port): `Provision` sets `pathEnabled: true` on `docker-hosted` and `docker-proxy` (lifted on the real bench, 2026-11-17). (5) TLS verification is disabled only for references whose host is `127.0.0.1` or `::1`, and only with a mandatory path. `seed` refuses a `--rest-host` that is not loopback. Credentials go through a temporary authfile (`0600` in a `0700` directory), never in argv. (6) The dataset is fixed in `dataset.go` and described in the descriptor: `api:1.0.0` and `api:1.1.0`, `team/api:1.0.0` and `tools/cli:0.1.0` in hosted; `shared/base:1.0` in hosted and in the upstream, with different content and digests; `mirror/cached:1.0` in the upstream, pulled once through the proxy; `mirror/uncached:1.0` in the upstream, never pulled. Each image carries a `org.devdesk.nexus-bench.source` label. (7) Absence is proven by a sentinel, `errImageAbsent`, returned only on a registry "manifest unknown"; any other failure stays an error, so an unreachable registry is never read as an empty one. (8) An unlisted digest is accepted. (9) Preflight checks the subresources the code uses (`kubectl auth can-i --subresource`: `deployments/scale`, `pods/portforward`), correcting an NX-01 defect that checked `pods/exec`. (10) `up` is extended: deploy Nexus and the upstream, bring the upstream to one ready replica, provision (the proxy is created and joins the group), seed, then write the descriptor, and only after full success. `seed` alone seeds. (11) The descriptor moves to schema 2 (`registryAccess`, `upstream`, `dataset`); `LoadDescriptor` rejects version 1. Rejected: a native Go registry client (it would share the bias of the client being measured); a public image from Docker Hub (network, mutable tags); an unauthenticated NodePort for the upstream (a writable registry on the LAN); `emptyDir` with reseeding (ad hoc content lost at each cut). (13) Nexus 3.98 enables an SSRF protection on a new installation and refuses a proxy whose remote URL host resolves to a private address, a ClusterIP included (HTTP 400 on `proxy.remoteUrl`). Decided on the real bench (2026-11-17): `Provision` adds the host of the bench upstream to `allowedDomains` of `/v1/security/ssrf-protection` before it creates the proxy, keeps the entries already there, never switches the protection on or off, and does nothing for an upstream URL the bench did not deploy (that refusal is explained with the endpoint to edit). Narrower than turning the protection off. (12) The story is split: NX-02a is DEVDESK-505 (upstream, proxy created, seed, descriptor; CA1, CA2, CA3, CA5, CA6); NX-02b is DEVDESK-534 (cut and restore, reset, proxy unblock, repository deletion; CA4, CA7). Why: one client and one dataset for every later measure, without a cluster-wide exposure, and a cut that the proxy cannot mistake for an empty registry. No separate ADR: the decision is local to the bench and reversible. **Reserves:** lifted on the real bench on 2026-11-17 (see "Observed on the real bench (NX-02a)" in `docs/architecture/nexus-bench.md`): H-PATH (false by default, fixed by `pathEnabled`), H-PUSH, H-LAYER, H-PROXYHTTP, H-DNS, H-UPRUN, H-SCALE and H-COMPONENTS held; H-SSRF was a surprise (13). H-UNBLOCK, H-STALE, H-CUTDELAY and H-REPODEL belong to NX-02b and are still assumptions. The duplicate `shared/base:1.0` has two digests. `descriptor.json` is versioned at schema 2. **Plane:** DEVDESK-505 (NX-02a), DEVDESK-534 (NX-02b). Sources: `.claude/plans/DEVDESK-505-plan.md`, `internal/nexusbench/`, `docs/product/nexus-support/stories.md` (NX-02); D157.**
+
 **D157 — DEVDESK-504 (NX-01): a disposable Nexus Community bench on the datomir k0s cluster, driven by a Go tool outside `ci`. Decided (user, 2026-11-16, plan `.claude/plans/DEVDESK-504-plan.md`, drafted there under this number): (1) The tool is `internal/nexusbench` with its command `cmd/nexus-bench`, reached only through the tasks `nexus-bench:up`, `provision`, `down`, `status`, `render` and `test` of `mise.toml`. None is reachable from `ci`, `check`, `test` or `test-race`, and `mise_tasks_test.go` checks it. Each task declares kubectl `1.35` itself, not in the global `[tools]`; `render` contacts no cluster and declares none. (2) kubectl is driven as a process (`kubectl.go`), not through `client-go`. (3) Manifests are embedded `text/template` files, with the values validated before rendering. The namespace is `nexus-bench`, the NodePorts are 30481 (REST and UI) and 30482 (Docker connector), and 30483-30489 stays reserved for the next NX stories. (4) The descriptor is a versioned JSON file, `internal/nexusbench/descriptor.json`, written deterministically; it holds no secret. (5) Secrets stay out of the repository, in `$XDG_STATE_HOME/devdesk/nexus-bench/` (else `~/.local/state`, else the path given by `NEXUS_BENCH_SECRETS`), and the store refuses a path inside the repository (`secrets.go`). (6) REST credentials are sent to `127.0.0.1` by default. Another host needs `--rest-host`, and the command then warns that the credentials travel in clear text. (7) The Community EULA is accepted only when the operator sets `NEXUS_BENCH_ACCEPT_EULA=yes` (or `--accept-eula`); the REST client never accepts it on its own. (8) Destruction deletes the one namespace the tool labelled; `down` leaves the descriptor alone. (9) Integration tests are gated: `DEVDESK_NEXUS_BENCH=1` for the read-only tests, and additionally `DEVDESK_NEXUS_BENCH_LIFECYCLE=1` with `NEXUS_BENCH_ACCEPT_EULA=yes` for the destructive lifecycle; `nexus-bench:test` runs with `-timeout 60m`, and the lifecycle refuses to start with less than 45 minutes left. Measured on `datomir` (k0s v1.36, Nexus 3.98.1-01 Community) on 2026-11-16: the read-only tests and the lifecycle pass (lifecycle 151 s); a first `up` is Ready in 48 s; a second and third `up` are idempotent (no `POST` beyond the refused proxy, the same `descriptor.json` byte for byte). **Measured change, proxy deferred to NX-02:** Nexus refuses (`400`, `Proxy URL blocked: Failed to resolve host`) a Docker proxy whose upstream host does not resolve, and `bench-upstream` does not exist before NX-02. The bench therefore ships without `docker-proxy`, and `docker-group` holds `docker-hosted` alone. The versioned `descriptor.json` describes that state and differs from the one NX-02 will write (proxy present and listed in the group). Rejected: (a) bash scripts (not testable as units in this repository); (b) `client-go` (weight in the binary's `go.mod`); (c) ignoring the descriptor in git (NX-08 could no longer detect a changed version without the bench). Consequences: NX-02 deploys the upstream `bench-upstream` (port 5000) and a NodePort of the reserved block, then re-runs `mise run nexus-bench:up`, checks that `docker-proxy` exists and is in `docker-group`, and commits the new `descriptor.json`; NX-03 to NX-08 read the descriptor through `nexusbench.LoadDescriptor`. Residual risks, accepted for a throw-away bench, not fixed here (`docs/architecture/nexus-bench.md`, "Residual risks"): the Basic credentials travel in clear HTTP; the `local-path` data folder is `0777`, so `admin.password` is readable by every local account of the node until the password is changed; the NodePorts listen on every interface of the node with anonymous access on by default; the image tag is mutable (no digest pinned). Not addressed by this decision, known and accepted: (a) the namespace label is checked, then the namespace is deleted with no UID precondition; a namespace deleted and recreated without the label between the two steps could be removed (low risk, not handled); (b) the image is pinned by tag, not by digest, so the same tag can resolve to another image; (c) the `local-path` data folder is `0777` without `fsGroup`, so `admin.password` is readable by the local accounts of the node between the start of Nexus and the provisioning. **Plane:** DEVDESK-504 (story), DEVDESK-533 (NX-01). Sources: `.claude/plans/DEVDESK-504-plan.md`, `docs/architecture/nexus-bench.md` (decision D-B and following, "The command and the mise tasks", "Integration tests and source guards", "First real run", "Residual risks"), `internal/nexusbench/params.go`, `secrets.go`, `descriptor.json`.**

 **D156 — DEVDESK-412 (security): the registry HTTP clients DEVDESK-410 left out get the same origin and size hardening, and a template blob may be served from another origin, without credentials. Decided (user, 2026-11-16, questions of the plan `.claude/plans/DEVDESK-412-plan.md`): (Q1b) the layer blob of an OCI template follows a redirect to another host, without `Authorization` or `Referer` and without any retry with credentials. *This is an explicit exception to the story's "out of scope: CDN redirect support"*; every other call (template tags and manifest, registry tags, catalog, token, Nexus) keeps refusing an off-origin redirect (D142). (Q2) `oci.Client` has a 1 minute timeout for a whole request, redirects and body included. (Q3) an `http://` Bearer realm behind an `https` registry is refused, with or without credentials (this closes D142's residual risk of the clear-text realm); a realm that is neither `http` nor `https` is refused too. (Q4) the registry URL is redacted in `browser_bridge.go` and in the error prefixes of `registry.go` (`RedactURL`; closes D142's raw-URL residual risk). (Q5a) the digest of the layer is always checked, redirected or not: `sha256:` and 64 lowercase hex digits only, so a registry whose layers are `sha512` is refused. (Q6) no redirect from `https` to `http` (compared with the previous hop); the SSRF guard is loopback and link-local only (`localhost`, `*.localhost`, `0.0.0.0` and literal addresses; private ranges are not filtered). What shipped: `internal/oci/guard.go` exports `MaxRedirects`, `ErrOffOriginRedirect`, `ErrTooManyRedirects`, `RefuseOffOriginRedirect`, `StripRedirectTarget`, `ErrBodyTooLarge`, `ReadAllLimited`, `RedactURL`, `RedactRegistryURL` (address with or without scheme, host kept readable; used by the `browser_bridge.go`, `registries.go` and `commands.go` logs; `redactGitURL` in `internal/template/fetch.go` masks a git source URL in `fetching ...` for `http(s)`, scp form unchanged, and also a token written as the user name once the coder's change lands) (the names D142 gave in lowercase); `internal/oci/oci_blob.go` holds the blob policy: 10 hops, `http` and `https` only, no `https` to `http`, refusal of loopback, link-local and unspecified targets unless the registry itself is local, `Authorization` and `Referer` deleted on every hop after the chain left the origin (sticky, because `net/http` copies them to the same host on another port and to a subdomain before `CheckRedirect` runs), `Dialer.Control` against DNS rebinding with an exception for the `ip:port` of the configured proxy, compressed cap `blobCap` (`Bytes + Files*2048 + 1 MiB`, none without a byte limit), errors naming the host only. Nexus: `CheckRedirect` and a 1 MiB read cap (not measured), and the anonymous retry no longer starts after a refused off-origin redirect. Docker Hub tag dates: 4 MiB cap, measured 2026-11-16: 530,629 bytes for `library/postgres` (the largest page seen). Incident of 2026-11-16 17:21: a test with an endless, uncapped reader exhausted the memory of the machine (OOM). Rule for tests that follows: never serve or read an endless body without a cap; bound it with a sentinel error (a reader that fails after N bytes, a handler that stops on the first write error), never rely on the timeout. Residual risks accepted (final, after the security review): (1) private networks are not filtered (RFC 1918, ULA fc00::/7 including the AWS IPv6 IMDS fd00:ec2::254, 100.64/10, Alibaba 100.100.100.200, Azure 168.63.129.16): a hostile registry can make DevDesk send a blind anonymous `GET` there; the answer is never sent back; (2) a local registry named by an alias (`/etc/hosts`, a DNS name to 127.0.0.1, such as `registry.lan`) is not recognized as local and its blob is refused at the dial: use `localhost`, `*.localhost` or a literal IP; (3) behind a proxy, the proxy resolves the target, so only the check of the literal name applies; (4) the proxy exception allows the `ip:port` of the configured proxy for every connection of the transport, not only those `Proxy()` decided: with `NO_PROXY`, a name that resolves to that port stays reachable directly, as an anonymous `GET`; (5) when the registry is local (`localhost`, loopback, link-local) no SSRF filter applies; (6) an `http` registry can redirect the blob to any `http` URL, bounded by the loopback and link-local filter when the registry is not local; (7) CPU: a very compressible entry or PAX headers can make the tar reader decompress up to about 34 GiB, bounded by the 1 minute timeout, before the digest check, with no memory cost; (8) the unverified tar.gz is parsed in memory before the digest check, bounded by `Limits`; nothing is returned or cached before the check; (9) the digest is limited to sha256 (a sha512 layer is refused); (10) integrity is that of the manifest (a mutable tag), with no signature verification; (11) a Bearer `https` realm may be on another host and receives the Basic credentials (standard Docker behavior); (12) Nexus refuses `http` to `https` on the same host (the scheme is part of the origin), so enter the URL in `https`; its 1 MiB cap is not measured; the anonymous retry starts on any error except the origin refusal; (13) `oci.Client.ListTags` decodes an unbounded body (unused in production), and 410's own bounded reads were not moved onto `ReadAllLimited`; (14) each `NewClient` clones a `Transport` whose idle connections stay about 90 s (a resource, not a leak). No ADR: local and reversible by a revert. **Plane:** DEVDESK-412 (story), DEVDESK-523 (412-9, docs), dated 2026-11-16. Sources: `.claude/plans/DEVDESK-412-plan.md`, `docs/architecture/network.md`, `internal/oci/guard.go`, `internal/oci/oci_blob.go`; D142, D149.**
